For the truth-table model:
```
# witness model: 3 geodesics, 2 points q1,q2
# sets: A1 = J^-(q1) intersect M, A2 = J^-(q2)
# gamma1: tail(gamma1) subset A1, gamma1 subset A1    -> P=T, S=T
# gamma2: gamma2 subset A1 ∪ A2, but no tail in A1 and no tail in A2 -> P=F, S=T
# gamma3: gamma3 NOT subset A1 ∪ A2                    -> P=F, S=F
P = lambda g: any(tail_subset(g,q) for q in (q1,q2))
S = lambda g: g <= (A1|A2)
```
Represent each gamma as a set of cells {early, late}. tail(gamma) = {late}. Need tail_subset(g,q): late ∈ A_q. gamma ⊆ A1∪A2.
- gamma1: cells {a1_early, a1_late}; A1={a1_early,a1_late}, A2={a2_early,a2_late}. P: late ∈ A1 ✓ → T. S ✓ → T.
- gamma2: cells {a1_early, a2_late}: S ✓. tail={a2_late}; is tail ⊆ A1? no. ⊆A2? yes! That would make P true. Hmm. Need a case where tail is in NO single A_q but curve is in the union. Since tail must be contained in one A_q, and the curve can straddle: gamma2 = {a1_early, a2_late}. tail={a2_late} ⊆ A2 → P true. Not good.

The omega-chain witness: I+ = {q_n}, J^-(q_n) increasing; gamma's tail is contained in ⋃_{n≥N} J^-(q_n) but not in any single J^-(q_n). To model finitely: q1, q2, q3 with A1 ⊂ A2 ⊂ A3, and the tail has points in A3 \ A2 (so not in A1 or A2) and also a point in A1? Let's construct:
- cells: e (early), m (middle), l (late)
- A1 = {e}
- A2 = {e, m}
- A3 = {e, m, l}
- gamma2 = {e, m, l}? tail = {m, l}: ⊆A3 yes → P true. Hmm.

The point of the omega-chain: for each q_n, part of the tail escapes J^-(q_n). Finitely: tail has segments; segment_i ⊆ A_i but not A_{i-1}; then tail ⊄ A_j for any single j because it has a segment in A_{i} for i>j... With nested A's, tail ⊆ A_3 (the largest) → P true again. The actual omega-chain uses non-nested or unbounded chain: q_n with J^-(q_n) not containing a common tail. So model: A1={e, m1}; A2={e, m2}; A3 = {e, m1, m2} (this is the union). tail = {m1, m2}: ⊆ A3 yes → P true. Ugh, because I included the union as a single A3.

Better: choose A_i such that no single A_i contains the whole tail, but the union does:
- A1 = {e, m1}
- A2 = {e, m2}
- union = {e, m1, m2}
- gamma2 = {e, m1, m2}; tail = {m1,m2} ⊄ A1 (missing m2), ⊄ A2 (missing m1) → P false. gamma2 ⊆ union → S true. 
So with q1,q2 only: A1={e,m1}, A2={e,m2}. gamma2={e,m1,m2}. S true, P false. 
- gamma1={e,m1}: tail={m1} ⊆ A1 → P true; S true.
- gamma3={x} with x outside union → P false, S false.
Now: P⟹S holds (γ1 only P-true). S⇏P witnessed by γ2. ¬S⟹¬P (γ3). ¬P⇏¬S (γ2). 

And class statements over this model:
- parent conclusion ∀γ¬P: true iff no γ has P. In our model γ1 has P, so false.
- variant conclusion ∀γ¬S: false (γ1,γ2 have S).
